[Event "Black openings: he plays the king pawn"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/InPnxGYF"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis"]
[Role "teaching"]

{ Today you are Black, and today's word is the centre: the four squares in the middle of the board. Whatever he grabs there, you grab back. }
1. e4 { Out comes his king pawn, two squares into the centre. Do not leave it all to him: push yours out to meet it, nose to nose. [%cal Ye7e5] } 1... e5 { Nose to nose in the centre. Pawns capture diagonally, so these two can neither take each other nor squeeze past. [%csl Ge5] } (1... c5 { The Sicilian: he takes one half of the centre and you grab at the other. A fine opening, and a jungle for another day. } 2. Nf3 { His knight comes out and a thousand sharp paths open up. Today we answer him in the centre instead. }) (1... e6 { The French. Solid, but that pawn shuts in your light bishop, and it can sit at home for a very long time. } 2. d4 d5 { A real defence and a chapter of its own. Ours meets his pawn face to face rather than hiding behind one. }) (1... c6 { The Caro-Kann. Also good: the little pawn gets ready to hit the centre next move and leaves your light bishop free. } 2. d4 d5 { Careful, solid chess. Today we go for the centre with the king pawn instead. }) (1... d5 { Bold, and he simply takes. Winning the pawn back means your queen coming out on move two, where she gets chased. } 2. exd5 { A pawn down. Your queen can fetch it, and then his knight jumps out and shoos her about the board. }) 2. Nf3 { His knight jumps out and leans on your centre pawn. Guard it with a piece you wanted to develop anyway. [%cal Yb8c6] } 2... Nc6 { Your knight guards the pawn and comes out to play in the same move. That is the cheapest defending there is. [%cal Gc6e5] } (2... Nf6 { The Petrov, and it is perfectly good. It ignores his threat and pokes at his centre pawn instead. } 3. Nxe5 d6 4. Nf3 { He grabs your pawn, you kick his knight home, and your knight eats his pawn back. A whole lesson of its own. }) (2... d6 { Solid, and it locks your dark bishop in behind its own pawn. That bishop will be waiting a long while. } 3. d4 exd4 4. Nxd4 { He owns the centre, his pieces have room, and everything you own is squashed into the three rows nearest you. }) (2... f6 { Never guard with this pawn. It rips open the road to your own king, and there were two better guards available. } 3. Nxe5 { He takes anyway. Take back and his queen checks, collects your centre pawn and then the rook in the corner. }) (2... d5 { The elephant gambit: a pawn offered to open lines. He just takes it, and far too little comes back. } 3. exd5 e4 4. Qe2 { He is a pawn up, and the pawn you shoved forward is stuck in front of your own king with nothing guarding it. }) 3. Bc4 { The Italian. His bishop aims at the red square beside your king, the one your king alone is guarding. Copy him. [%csl Rf7][%cal Yf8c5] } 3... Bc5 { Your bishop glares at the sore square beside his king, and his king is its only guard too. Even game, even chances. [%cal Gc5f2] } (3... Nf6 { The Two Knights, and it is worth every bit as much as ours. It does invite a very rude knight jump. } 4. Ng5 { Straight at the square only your king guards. That is the fried liver attack, and surviving it is a lesson of its own. }) (3... d6 { Safe and sleepy. The pawn shuts in your dark bishop, and that bishop had the best square on the board waiting. } 4. O-O Be7 { Playable, but his bishop owns the long angry diagonal and yours is looking at the back of a pawn. }) (3... Be7 { The bishop steps out to a quiet square and hands him the whole centre. Nothing is lost, and nothing is fought for either. } 4. d4 exd4 { His pawns own the centre, he wins the pawn straight back, and your pieces have nothing to bite on. }) (3... Na5 { The knight chases his bishop, but it lands on the edge and stops guarding your centre pawn. } 4. Nxe5 { He snaps up the free pawn. You do win his bishop, and he stays a pawn up and perfectly happy. }) 4. c3 { He props up a pawn push into the centre. Bring your last sleeping knight out and let it stare at his king pawn. [%cal Yg8f6] } 4... Nf6 { Out it comes, and his king pawn is suddenly being watched. Every move should do a job like that. [%cal Gf6e4] } (4... Bb6 { The bishop tiptoes backwards before anybody has asked it to, and he gets a free move to build. } 5. d4 d6 { His two pawns stand side by side in the centre, and you spent your move shuffling. }) (4... d6 { Playable and slow. It does prop up your centre pawn, but your last knight was itching to come out. } 5. d4 Bb6 { He rolls both centre pawns forward and your bishop has to step out of their way. }) (4... Qe7 { Solid, and a little clumsy: your queen sits directly behind her own centre pawn, where she is in her own way. } 5. d4 Bb6 { Two of his pawns fill the centre, and your queen spends the opening staring at the back of her own pawn. }) (4... Bxf2+ { A check, and it costs a whole bishop to give it. His king simply takes, and nothing follows. } 5. Kxf2 { His king eats the bishop and stands there quite happily. You gave a bishop and collected one pawn. }) 5. d3 { He props his king pawn up with the queen pawn. Do the very same thing and your light bishop gets a road out. [%cal Yd7d6] } 5... d6 { The little pawn guards your centre pawn and opens the long road for your light bishop at the same time. [%cal Gc8h3] } (5... O-O { Castling is good too, and we play it next move anyway. The only question is which useful move comes first. } 6. O-O d6 { The very same position, reached by swapping two moves round. Nothing gained and nothing lost. }) (5... a6 { A handy little move: it takes a square away from his pieces and gives your bishop a corner to retreat to. } 6. O-O Ba7 { Fine chess. We open the road for the light bishop first and leave the other one where it is. }) (5... Bb6 { The bishop backs off before a single pawn has come near it. No harm done, but a whole move spent on nothing. } 6. O-O d6 { The pawn move turns up anyway, one move later than it needed to. }) (5... Ng4 { The knight leaps at the pawn beside his king. Two of your pieces hit it, his king guards it, and taking loses a piece. } 6. d4 { He kicks your bishop and takes the centre while your knight sits out there with nothing to do. }) 6. O-O { He tucks his king into the corner behind three pawns. Your king has waited long enough: send him to his own corner. [%cal Ye8g8] } 6... O-O { Both kings are tucked away, three of your pieces are out, and nothing of yours is hanging. A perfect opening. [%csl Gg8] } (6... a6 { A genuinely useful little move, and still second in the queue. Your king should go home before anything else. } 7. a4 O-O { You get castled one move later, and he grabbed some space on that wing while you waited. }) (6... h6 { It keeps his pieces off the square beside your king, but your king is still standing in the centre. } 7. b4 Bb6 { He grabs space on the other wing and pushes your bishop about while your king waits. }) (6... Bg4 { Your bishop leans on his knight, and his queen stands right behind it, so that knight dare not move. He shrugs it off. } 7. Nbd2 { He brings the last knight out, a pawn will poke your bishop away, and your king is still homeless. }) (6... Bb6 { The bishop retreats with nobody chasing it, and your king is still in the centre. A move spent on nothing. } 7. Nbd2 h6 { He finishes developing in peace while you play quiet pawn moves and leave your king where he stands. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the Spanish, he takes the knight"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/FmyET55b"]
[FEN "r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R w KQkq - 2 3"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/r1bqkbnr/pppp1ppp/2n5/4p3/4P3/5N2/PPPP1PPP/RNBQKB1R_w_KQkq_-_2_3"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ The same opening, and you each own half of the centre. This time keep your eye on his light bishop: it has found a new target. }
3. Bb5 { The Spanish. His bishop leans on the knight that is your centre pawn's only guard. Make that bishop say what it wants. [%csl Rc6] } 3... a6 { The little pawn asks the big question: take the knight, or step back? He has to answer it right now. [%cal Ga6b5] } (3... Nf6 { The Berlin, and it is every bit as good. That bishop is not really threatening anything yet, so you simply develop. } 4. O-O Nxe4 { You help yourself to his centre pawn on the way. A huge, serious opening; ours makes the bishop decide first. }) (3... Bc5 { A natural developing move and perfectly playable: the bishop comes out to an active square pointing at his king. } 4. c3 Nf6 { A good game for you. In our line the bishop is made to choose before anything else happens. }) (3... Be7 { Safe and quiet. That bishop had a busier square available, and this move asks him nothing whatsoever. } 4. c3 Nf6 { You are absolutely fine, and he gets an easy afternoon with no awkward questions to answer. }) (3... Nge7 { This knight comes out in front of your own dark bishop and shuts it in for a move or two. } 4. O-O Ng6 { Playable, and the knight untangles. It has taken two moves to reach a square that one move usually buys. }) (3... d6 { Solid, and a little passive: the pawn locks your dark bishop in behind it. } 4. Nc3 Nf6 { Perfectly playable. He has the easier game, because nothing he owns has been questioned yet. }) 4. Bxc6 { He takes the guard away, so your centre pawn is loose. Two of your pawns can take back. Which one, and why? [%csl Re5] } 4... dxc6 { The queen pawn takes, and now your queen looks straight down an open column at his half of the board. Let him grab that pawn if he dares. [%csl Gd8] } (4... bxc6 { The wing pawn takes instead, so your queen pawn stays put and blocks her road to the centre. [%csl Rd7] } 5. Nc3 Qf6 6. d4 { With no double attack to fear he leaves your centre pawn alone and builds a big pawn centre instead. }) (4... Nf6 { A good developing move that forgets his bishop is still sitting there, unpaid for, on your knight's square. } 5. Bd5 c6 6. Bb3 { The bishop strolls calmly away to safety and he is a whole knight up for nothing at all. }) (4... Bc5 { A fine square for the bishop, and his bishop is still standing where your knight used to be. } 5. Ba4 Nf6 6. Nxe5 { A knight and a pawn down. Win the piece back first, then develop in peace. }) 5. Nxe5 { He grabs the loose pawn, and that is his mistake: castling was the move. Now go and win the pawn straight back. [%csl Re5,Re4] } 5... Qd4 { Out she comes to the centre, hitting his knight and the pawn right behind it. He cannot save both. [%cal Gd4e5,Gd4e4] } (5... Qe7 { Just as good, and just as sneaky: your queen lines up on the knight and the pawn behind it from the other side. } 6. Nf3 Qxe4+ { His knight runs, you take the pawn with check, and you land in exactly the position our move reaches. }) (5... Qg5 { Greedy in the other direction: your queen goes after the pawn beside his king instead. It is playable. } 6. Nf3 Qxg2 7. Rg1 { You have a pawn back and material is level, but now his rook chases your queen round the garden. }) (5... Nf6 { A good square for the knight, and it quietly forgets that he is a pawn up and the pawn is still catchable. } 6. d3 Bc5 7. Nf3 { He props the pawn up, tucks his knight back home, and you have nothing at all to show for the pawn. }) (5... Bd6 { You develop politely and let him keep his snack. That pawn had to be won back at once or never. } 6. Nc4 Bc5 7. d3 { He is a clean pawn up and nothing of yours attacks a thing. Politeness cost you a pawn. }) (5... Be6 { The bishop comes out to a sensible square, and the pawn he helped himself to is gone for good. } 6. d4 Ne7 7. Be3 { A pawn down with no way of winning it back. The moment to strike was the moment he grabbed it. }) 6. Nf3 { His knight runs from your queen. It had to move, and that leaves his centre pawn standing there with nobody guarding it. } 6... Qxe4+ { The pawn is back and the check comes free. You own two bishops now and he owns one. [%csl Gc8,Gf8] } (6... Qf6 { Your queen steps aside and the free pawn in the centre stays his. It was hanging, and it will not hang twice. } 7. d4 Qg6 8. Nc3 { His knight guards the pawn, his centre is huge, and your queen trip has bought you precisely nothing. }) (6... Qd6 { Safe enough, and the one free pawn on the board goes untouched. Taking it was the whole idea of the trip. } 7. d3 Ne7 8. Nbd2 { He props the pawn up with another pawn, stays a pawn ahead and has a comfortable, easy game. }) (6... Qd8 { All the way home again, and the journey was for nothing: the pawn she came out for is still standing there. } 7. d4 Ne7 8. Qd3 { He is a pawn up with both centre pawns side by side, and your queen is back where she started. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the Spanish main line"]
[Result "0-1"]
