[Role "exercise"]

{ You met him in the centre again, pawn for pawn. This time watch what he throws at you on move two, and do not panic. }
2. Qh5 { Out comes his queen on move two, glaring at your centre pawn, which has nobody guarding it. Answer her properly. } 2... Nc6 { Guard and develop in a single move. That is how you answer a bully: bring out a piece that does two jobs. [%csl Ge5] } (2... g6 { Never shoo her with that pawn. It does not guard your centre pawn, so she simply helps herself to it. } 3. Qxe5+ { Check, and a double attack: your king and the rook sitting in the corner behind it. [%cal Re5h8] }) (2... Nf6 { It develops, and the pawn drops with check. Your pieces come out so fast that the game stays perfectly playable. } 3. Qxe5+ Be7 { A pawn down, and every move you make now chases her. Ours guards the pawn instead. }) (2... d6 { This guards the pawn too, and it is every bit as good. It does shut your dark bishop in for a while. } 3. Bc4 g6 { A fine game. Our knight guards and develops at the same time, which is one job more. }) (2... Qe7 { Solid, and your queen really does guard the pawn. She also stands in her own bishop's doorway. } 3. Bc4 Nf6 { Playable, and a shade awkward: your bishop has to wait for the queen to move again. }) (2... Bd6 { It guards the pawn, and it stands on your own queen pawn's toes so that pawn cannot move. } 3. Bc4 Qe7 { Cramped, with your pieces treading on each other, and he is slightly the better placed. }) 3. Bc4 { Now his bishop joins her, and both of them stare at the red square beside your king. That is the four move mate. Stop it. [%csl Rf7] } 3... g6 { The little pawn shoos the queen away and shuts the door on the sore square beside your king at the same time. [%cal Gg6h5] } (3... Nf6 { The most natural move on the board, and it walks straight into mate. } 4. Qxf7# { His queen lands beside your king with the bishop guarding her, so nothing can take her. Scholar's mate. }) (3... d5 { It pokes his bishop, and his bishop simply takes the pawn. Your knight is the next thing on that diagonal. } 4. Bxd5 Qe7 5. Bxc6+ { He takes the pawn, then the knight with check, and once you take back he is a clean pawn up. }) (3... Qe7 { Good defending: your queen guards the sore square beside your king and nothing is hanging. } 4. Qd1 Nf6 { He gives up and sends her home. A perfectly good game, and your queen did the guarding. }) (3... Nh6 { That knight guards the sore square beside your king, and it is a miserable piece out on the edge. } 4. d3 g6 { The pawn shoos her away in the end, and now you are stuck with a knight out on the rim. }) 4. Qf3 { She slides across and takes aim at the red square all over again, with the bishop still pointing there too. Stop the mate. [%csl Rf7] } 4... Nf6 { Your knight steps into the doorway and develops while it is there. Her big idea is dead. [%csl Gf6] } (4... Bg7 { A lovely square for the bishop, and it leaves the road to your king wide open. } 5. Qxf7# { The queen drops in beside your king with the bishop propping her up. The road had to be blocked. }) (4... Qe7 { Your queen guards the green square, and that is perfectly good defending too. [%csl Gf7] } 5. Ne2 Na5 { You are fine, and you chase his bishop into the bargain. The mate never had a chance. }) (4... Qf6 { Offering a swap of queens. Fine, and your queen has now moved twice to arrange it. } 5. Qd1 Qd8 { He declines, both queens go home, and the two of you are exactly where you started. }) (4... Nh6 { It stops the mate, and it parks your knight on the edge where it does the least work. } 5. Ne2 Na5 { Perfectly playable, and the other knight was the one that wanted that job. }) (4... f5 { It blocks her road to your king, and it pulls the pawn away from in front of your own king. } 5. Ne2 Nf6 { Still perfectly fine, and you have a draughty door to keep an eye on for a long time. }) 5. Ne2 { His knight finally comes out. Two queen trips, and all he has to show for them is your pawn on a useful square. } 5... Bg7 { Your bishop fills the square the g pawn left behind. Three pieces out, nothing hanging, and his raid has won him nothing. [%csl Gg7] } (5... d6 { Just as good, maybe even a shade more precise: it opens the road for your other bishop too. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { The same bishop move, one beat later, and your position is a picture of health. }) (5... h6 { A quiet little pawn move while your bishop is still waiting to come out. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { The bishop comes out in the end, and he used your quiet move to bring another piece into the game. }) (5... Na5 { Going after his bishop. Perfectly playable, and a little slow when a piece is still at home. } 6. d3 Bg7 { The bishop was the piece that mattered, and it comes out one move later. }) (5... a5 { A pawn step out on the wing with no plan behind it. Nothing breaks, and nothing improves either. } 6. Nbc3 Bg7 { You put the bishop on its square at last, one move later than you needed to. }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: he plays the queen pawn"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
