[Role "teaching"]

{ Now for his other big opening move. Today's new word is the pin, and he is going to try one on you before this game is out. }
1. d4 { He pushes the queen pawn instead. Same plan, other half of the centre: your queen pawn marches out to meet it. [%cal Yd7d5] } 1... d5 { Face to face again, and neither pawn can get past the other. Nobody is handing over the centre today. } (1... Nf6 { Excellent too, and the most flexible move on the board. It also opens an enormous book of its own. } 2. c4 g6 { That is the Indian world, full of sharp ideas. Today we keep it simple and answer him pawn for pawn. }) (1... e6 { Playable, and it can even turn into our line. It does shut your light bishop in before it has ever been out. } 2. e4 d5 { And there you are in the French, arriving through the back door. }) (1... c6 { A waiting move, and a decent one. That little pawn would rather be propping up a pawn in the centre. } 2. e3 d5 { You push the queen pawn out anyway, one move later than you needed to. }) (1... d6 { Too small a step. He takes the rest of the centre with his king pawn and you have to fight your way in. } 2. e4 Nf6 { He has two fat pawns side by side and you are poking at them from underneath. }) 2. c4 { The queen's gambit: he offers a pawn beside his own to lure your centre pawn away from the middle. Prop yours up. [%cal Ye7e6] } 2... e6 { Propped up. Your centre pawn is staying exactly where it is, and your dark bishop now has a door to walk through. } (2... dxc4 { The queen's gambit accepted, and it is perfectly sound. You will not be allowed to hold on to the pawn. } 3. e4 { Two big pawns take the centre for him, and he collects your extra pawn back at his leisure. }) (2... c6 { The Slav. A very good move: it props the centre pawn up and leaves your light bishop its diagonal. } 3. Nf3 Nf6 { Solid, serious stuff, and a whole chapter of its own. Ours props the centre up with the king pawn. }) (2... Nc6 { This knight takes the square your own c pawn wants, and now that pawn cannot move at all. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. e3 { His centre is bigger than yours and your queen came out on move three. }) (2... c5 { Very sharp and very early. He takes in the centre and your queen has to come out and fetch the pawn back. } 3. cxd5 Qxd5 4. Nf3 { His pieces fly out and your queen has to watch for a knight jumping up to hit her. }) 3. Nc3 { He develops and leans on your centre pawn. Send out the knight that helps to guard it. [%cal Yg8f6] } 3... Nf6 { Out it comes, and your centre pawn has a friend. Three of yours guard it now and two of his attack it. } (3... c6 { The Semi-Slav, every bit as good as ours and a chapter of its own. The centre pawn gets one more guard. } 4. e3 Nf6 { You bring the knight out anyway, and he has built his little pawn wall in the meantime. }) (3... Bb4 { Just as good: your bishop leans on the knight that is leaning on your centre. It is a sharper road. } 4. e3 Nf6 { The same knight move by a rougher path, and the game turns messy much faster. }) (3... c5 { The sharp break, and every bit as good as ours. It leaves you with a centre pawn standing all alone. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 { A real opening in its own right: that lone pawn is strong and a target at the same time. }) (3... a6 { A slow little move while the centre is still being argued over. Nothing broken, nothing gained. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Nf3 { He is comfortable and developing, and your pawn move did no work at all. }) (3... Be7 { Fine, just one move early: the knight usually comes first, because it guards the centre pawn. } 4. cxd5 exd5 5. Bf4 { Playable for you, and his bishop throws the first punch from outside his pawns. }) 4. Bg5 { The pin: his bishop hits your knight, and your queen stands right behind it, so moving the knight would lose her. Block the road. [%cal Rg5d8,Yf8e7] } 4... Be7 { Your bishop steps into the gap between your knight and your queen. The knight can move again and the pin is dead. [%cal Ge7g5] } (4... dxc4 { Grabbing the pawn here is also playable, and people play it on purpose. } 5. e3 c5 { A serious line, and a great deal more to remember than ours. }) (4... Bb4 { A pin against a pin: your bishop leans on the knight that holds his centre together. Sharp, and just as good. } 5. e3 c5 { Playable and fighting. Ours is the calm road, and a calm road is easier to steer. }) (4... c6 { Solid and a touch passive: it props the centre up and leaves the pinned knight to look after itself. } 5. e3 Nbd7 { Fine, and you end up bringing a piece over to guard that knight in any case. }) (4... Nbd7 { Also solid, and rather clever: the second knight guards the pinned one, which is exactly the problem. } 5. cxd5 exd5 { Playable and comfortable. We answer the pin by filling the gap with the bishop instead. }) 5. e3 { He builds a small, solid wall for his pieces to stand behind. Your king has waited long enough: send him to the corner. [%cal Ye8g8] } 5... O-O { Your king is tucked in the corner, your knight and bishop are out, the pin is answered and nothing hangs. A fine opening. [%csl Gg8] } (5... h6 { Asking the bishop the question first is very good indeed: he has to choose between keeping the pin and swapping. } 6. Bh4 O-O { He keeps it, you castle, and you have gained a handy little pawn move for free. }) (5... Nbd7 { A good square and just as good a move, and your king is still standing about in the centre. } 6. Nf3 O-O { You reach the same safe position by a slightly different road. }) (5... b6 { Fine, and a little slow with your king still in the middle of the board. } 6. cxd5 Nxd5 { Playable, and he is a shade better with the freer pieces. }) (5... dxc4 { Taking now hands his bishop the square it wanted, and a free move to get there. } 6. Bxc4 a6 { His bishop comes out and takes a step forward in the same move. Why help him along? }) 0-1


[Event "Black openings: the London bishop"]
[Result "0-1"]
[Variant "Standard"]
[ChapterURL "https://lichess.org/study/4uudbvms/w8eQu5SA"]
[FEN "rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR w KQkq d6 0 2"]
[SetUp "1"]
[Site "https://lichess.org/analysis/rnbqkbnr/ppp1pppp/8/3p4/3P4/8/PPP1PPPP/RNBQKBNR_w_KQkq_d6_0_2"]
[Role "exercise"]

{ You have met him in the centre, pawn for pawn. Now watch his dark bishop come out early, with no pin in it for him this time. }
2. Bf4 { The London. He lets that bishop out before his pawns can shut it in, and he plays it against almost anything. [%csl Rf4] } 2... Nf6 { Your knight comes out, guards the square beside his bishop and keeps every one of your options open. [%cal Gf6e4] } (2... c5 { A fine punch at his centre pawn, and every bit as good as ours. It does invite a lot of early swapping. } 3. e3 Nf6 { Also good. We bring a piece out first and keep the position simple to steer. }) (2... e6 { Playable, and it shuts your light bishop in a little earlier than it needs to be. } 3. e3 c5 { Fine chess. Developing the knight first leaves that bishop one more door. }) (2... Bf5 { Also fine: this bishop gets outside your own pawns before they can lock it in behind them. } 3. c4 e6 { A real line, and he pokes at your centre pawn straight away. }) (2... Nc6 { Natural, and it takes the square your own c pawn wants, so that pawn cannot move at all. } 3. e3 Nf6 { He is comfortable, and one of your pawns is frozen for as long as the knight sits there. }) 3. e3 { Up goes his little pyramid, with the bishop safely outside it. Your dark bishop is still shut in behind your own pawns. [%csl Rf8] } 3... e6 { Your pawn steps up and the road in front of your dark bishop opens, so it can go and look his in the eye. [%csl Gf8] } (3... c5 { That pawn punches straight at his centre, and it is every bit as good as ours. } 4. Nf3 Nc6 { Also fine. Going after his best bishop is our idea instead. }) (3... g6 { A different plan and quite playable: the bishop goes out to the wing instead. } 4. c3 c5 { Fine chess, but from there your bishop stares past his rather than meeting it. }) (3... Bf5 { The bishop comes out before the pawn, which is a real idea. Here he offers a swap of the two light ones. } 4. Bd3 Bxd3 5. cxd3 { Your best placed piece has gone, and the column his pawn left is a road for his rook. }) (3... Bg4 { There is nothing to pin: a pin needs something worth more standing behind, and his knight is still at home. } 4. Qd3 { His queen heads for the wing your bishop has just stopped guarding, with a check on the way if you let her. }) 4. Nf3 { His knight comes out. That dark bishop is the piece he is proudest of, sitting outside his pawns on a free diagonal. [%csl Rf4] } 4... Bd6 { Offer him the swap: his favourite bishop for the one of yours that was stuck at home. What a deal. [%cal Gd6f4] } (4... c5 { A fine move, hitting his centre at once, and it is worth exactly as much as ours. } 5. c3 Nc6 { Also good. Trading his proudest piece off the board is our idea. }) (4... Be7 { Safe, and it lets his favourite bishop live a long and very happy life. } 5. c4 O-O { Perfectly playable, and he keeps the better of the two dark bishops. }) (4... a6 { A little waiting move. Nothing wrong with it, and nothing whatever gained. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { He brings another piece out in peace while you shuffle a pawn. }) (4... Bd7 { This bishop has nothing at all to do on that square, and it blocks your own knight's way out. } 5. Nbd2 c5 { Playable, but the other bishop was the one with a job to do. }) 5. Bxd6 { He takes at once. Two different pieces of yours can take back, and they lead to two very different games. } 5... cxd6 { Your c pawn takes, so your centre pawns stand one behind the other and the column beside your queen is open. [%csl Gd6,Gd5] } (5... Qxd6 { Also completely fine, and worth exactly as much as ours. Your queen takes a good central square. } 6. c4 dxc4 { A perfectly good game. You help yourself to the pawn he pushes, and he comes and fetches it back later. }) (5... Ne4 { You left his bishop standing on the square where it had just eaten yours. } 6. Ba3 { The bishop strolls away to safety and you are a whole piece down for nothing. }) (5... Nc6 { Developing is lovely, and his bishop is still sitting there with your bishop in its pocket. } 6. Ba3 { A whole bishop gone, and he did not even have to fight for it. }) 0-1
